PostBar (Canada Post CPC four-state bar code) — format provenance and usability
==========================================================================

This file records where the PostBar symbology comes from, what governs it,
and the basis on which this project implements and redistributes it.

It is an ENGINEERING INVENTORY, NOT LEGAL ADVICE. Items marked [TO VERIFY]
have not been independently confirmed by the author.


ORIGIN AND SPECIFICATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

"PostBar" is Canada Post Corporation's (CPC) name for its four-state
(height-coded) bar code family. Canada Post's own engineering specification
for PostBar is not freely published — it is available only on request from
Canada Post. This project does NOT hold or rely on that document. Instead,
this implementation is built entirely from US Patent 5,602,382A ("Multiple
Bar Code Processing," Canada Post Corporation, filed 31 October 1994,
granted 11 February 1997), which discloses the complete symbol tables,
field layouts, and Reed-Solomon parameters in ordinary patent-disclosure
detail (not merely describing that the format exists).

An earlier research pass reported the patent's own tables as unreliably
extractable: two independent automated fetches of the same patent (Google
Patents' rendered page and a separate source) produced conflicting,
internally-inconsistent digit tables. This project's own `pdftotext
-layout` pass on the same PDF also produced garbled, column-merged output.
Direct inspection resolved this: the patent's actual scanned pages,
rendered to images and read directly, are completely legible and internally
consistent. The unreliability was in the automated text/OCR extraction
pipelines, not in the primary source itself — the same lesson this
project's KarTrak ACI implementation drew from a transposed Wikipedia
table, one level further (sometimes even a "raw text" layer needs to be
bypassed in favour of the actual rendered page).

Every table and field layout in this implementation was verified against
the patent's own fully worked PostBar.C10 example (DCI=Z, postal code
K1S 5B6, machine ID DHAH): this implementation reproduces the patent's
stated 16-symbol Reed-Solomon codeword
(`18 29 12 5 14 27 52 54 4 6 8 33 9 6 20 41`) and the patent's own stated
bar sequence exactly, symbol for symbol — not merely a self-consistent
round trip. The PostBar.D22 and PostBar.G12 field layouts were read
directly from the patent's own field tables and cross-checked for
internal arithmetic consistency (bar counts summing to the stated totals,
Reed-Solomon (n,k) parameters matching the stated message/parity symbol
counts); PostBar.D22's own worked example (postal code L3B 4T9, address
locator 1420, customer information CFFMIPLXF6V) round-trips correctly
through this implementation as an additional check.

Specification TEXT (the patent document) is a matter of public record —
patent disclosures are published precisely so the invention they describe
can be understood and, once any patent term expires, freely practiced.
This project implements the SYMBOLOGY the patent discloses (a system of
tables and field layouts, not a work of authorship in the copyright
sense); it does not reproduce, redistribute or excerpt the patent
document itself.

Other formats named in the patent — PostBar.D07, D12, G22, S06, S11 — use
Reed-Solomon field layouts not fully read and verified by this project and
are NOT implemented here. Implementing them would need the same direct,
worked-example-verified treatment given to C10/D22/G12, not an assumption
that the pattern generalizes.


PATENT STATUS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

US Patent 5,602,382A was filed 31 October 1994 and granted 11 February
1997. Under the standard 20-years-from-filing US patent term, it expired
around 31 October 2014, more than a decade before this implementation.
[TO VERIFY] No evidence of a later, still-active continuation or
divisional patent covering the same symbol tables was found during
research; the "available on request" barrier to Canada Post's own current
engineering specification is a separate, purely practical distribution
question (see ORIGIN above), not a patent-term one.


TRADEMARK
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

"PostBar" and "Canada Post" are understood to be trademarks or trade names
of Canada Post Corporation. [TO VERIFY] No trademark search was carried
out. A trademark does not restrict implementing a symbology; it restricts
branding — how a product names and presents itself. This project's
`postbarc10`/`postbard22`/`postbarg12` format ids and documentation use
the name descriptively, to identify the symbology being implemented, not
as a product brand or a claim of Canada Post endorsement or certification.


IMPLEMENTATION BASIS IN THIS PROJECT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

The 'A'/'N'/'Z' character tables, the field layouts for PostBar.C10, D22
and G12, and the Reed-Solomon parameters (defining roots, message/parity
symbol counts) are original Sythos data, transcribed directly from the
patent's page images and independently verified against its own worked
numerical examples as described above — not copied from any other
implementation (none is known to exist publicly; BWIPP, Zint and ZXing
were checked and none implement PostBar). Reed-Solomon arithmetic reuses
this project's existing, already-shipped `src/ts/core/galois-field.ts`
`GF64` constant (primitive polynomial X^6+X+1 over GF(2), exactly the
field the patent specifies) and `src/ts/core/reed-solomon.ts` generic
encoder/decoder, both already used elsewhere in this SDK (e.g. Aztec) —
reused as-is, not reimplemented a second time. No source code, table or
image asset from any other barcode implementation is copied or shipped.


CONCLUSION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

On the basis of the references below, PostBar (the C10/D22/G12 profiles
specifically) is understood to be freely implementable and redistributable:
its only detailed technical disclosure is a patent whose term has expired,
and the symbol tables and field layouts it discloses are systems of
encoding rules, not copyrightable expression. This project's implementation
of it is distributed under the MIT License with no additional restriction.

References relied upon:
  - US Patent 5,602,382A, "Multiple Bar Code Processing," Canada Post
    Corporation, filed 31 October 1994, granted 11 February 1997 —
    read directly from rendered page images, not automated text
    extraction (see ORIGIN above)
  - The patent's own fully worked PostBar.C10 example, reproduced exactly
    by this implementation as an independent verification, not merely a
    self-consistency check

This is an engineering assessment, not legal advice. Items marked
[TO VERIFY] have not been independently confirmed by the author. If your
use is commercially sensitive, obtain your own legal review.
